There are two distinct factors that made the accuracy numbers high:

1. The "Feature Leakage" (The 99.8% Accuracy in Version 3)
In the earlier version of the notebook (bug_prediction_v3.ipynb), the model achieved 99.8% (reported as 1.00) accuracy.

What caused it: The dataset included a column called confidence.
Why it was a leak: The confidence column had an extremely high correlation (-0.98) with the target column is_buggy. It was essentially acting as a "cheat sheet" or "answer key".
The solution: In Version 4, this column was dropped because a real-world system scanning newly pushed commits would not have a pre-filled confidence score. Dropping it forced the model to learn from actual code metrics (complexity, churn, time, developer history).
2. The "Class Imbalance" Effect (The 93.3% Accuracy without Leakage)
Even after removing the confidence cheat sheet, the overall system accuracy remains high at 93.3%.

What causes it: The dataset is heavily imbalanced—only 5.2% of commits are buggy, and 94.8% are safe.
The baseline trick: If a naive model predicts that every single commit is Safe, it will achieve 94.8% overall accuracy while failing to catch even a single bug.
Why overall accuracy drops to 93.3%: Because the model actively tries to catch bugs, it makes some false positive predictions (flagging safe commits as buggy). This lowers the overall accuracy slightly to 93.3%, but it successfully catches 47.4% of the actual bugs (Recall).
In short: Feature leakage (confidence) made it look perfect, while class imbalance keeps the overall accuracy high even when the model makes mistakes on the rare bug class.

